<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<omdoc base="latin:/fundamentals/equality.omdoc"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/equality.mmt#0.0.0:4330.105.0"/><meta property="http://cds.omdoc.org/?metadata?importedby"><om:OMOBJ xmlns:om="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath"><om:OMLIT type="http://www.openmath.org/cd?OpenMath?OMSTR" value="mmt-omdoc"/></om:OMOBJ></meta></metadata><instruction text="namespace latin:/"/><instruction text="import lfx http://gl.mathhub.info/MMT/LFX"/><instruction text="fixmeta http://cds.omdoc.org/urtheories/?LF"/><mref name="[latin:/?UntypedEquality]" target="latin:/?UntypedEquality"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/equality.mmt#81.5.0:102.5.21"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?UntypedEqualityND]" target="latin:/?UntypedEqualityND"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/equality.mmt#190.10.0:213.10.23"/></metadata></mref><opaque format="text"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/equality.mmt#742.26.0:908.28.93"/></metadata>Equality on proofs, mainly used for proof simplification in ./logic.

   As such it is the Curry-Howard analog of program execution / reduction in lambda calculi.</opaque><mref name="[latin:/?ProofEquality]" target="latin:/?ProofEquality"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/equality.mmt#910.29.0:929.29.19"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?UntypedEqualityNDS]" target="latin:/?UntypedEqualityNDS"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/equality.mmt#1018.34.0:1042.34.24"/></metadata></mref><opaque format="text"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/equality.mmt#1212.41.0:1661.46.0"/></metadata>A relative equality for a typed language, in which the equality of objects may depend on the type at which it is taken.
   This can be practical in connection with
   - record types: two records are equal if they agree in all fields required by the type
   - quotient types: equivalent representatives are equal if seen as elements of the quotient
   - forgetful functors and other implicit conversions: two different groups may be equal as sets</opaque><mref name="[latin:/?SoftTypedEquality]" target="latin:/?SoftTypedEquality"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/equality.mmt#1663.47.0:1686.47.23"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?SoftTypedEqualityND]" target="latin:/?SoftTypedEqualityND"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/equality.mmt#1817.53.0:1842.53.25"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?TypedEquality]" target="latin:/?TypedEquality"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/equality.mmt#2395.65.0:2414.65.19"/></metadata></mref><mref name="[latin:/?TypedEqualityND]" target="latin:/?TypedEqualityND"><metadata><link rel="http://cds.omdoc.org/mmt?metadata?sourceRef" resource="latin:/fundamentals/equality.mmt#2505.70.0:2526.70.21"/></metadata></mref></omdoc>